WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.740
- Graduates of the Class of 2020,

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.460
family members and friends.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.010
It's a privilege to be
able to speak with you,

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.412
even in this unusual way.

00:00:16.412 --> 00:00:20.170
I know you would rather be on
campus and I wish you could be

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:24.260
here, but I'm glad we can
all do our part to reduce

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.793
the transmission of
COVID-19 and stay healthy.

00:00:29.054 --> 00:00:33.160
Over the past several
months, the novel coronavirus

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.300
has spread around the globe,

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.103
infecting millions and
claiming far too many lives.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.713
It has come to nearly every community.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.803
It has come here to Yale's doorstep.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:53.220
This crisis has been a
wake-up call for all of us.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.703
It has stretched healthcare
systems to the breaking point.

00:00:58.596 --> 00:01:01.963
It has threatened economies,
both household and global.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.220
And like many tragedies, it
has exposed the vast gulf

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.343
between the most fortunate
and the most vulnerable.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.690
Long after the pandemic
itself has subsided,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:19.340
we will need to commit ourselves
to finding new and creative

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.873
solutions to these long-term problems.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.240
It may be too early to
say what we have learned

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.433
from all of this,

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:34.100
but at the least we see
more sharply than before

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.423
our interdependence as a human community.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.945
Our deep levels of connection
and need for one another

00:01:41.945 --> 00:01:46.150
are a weakness that
infectious diseases exploit,

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:49.380
but our interdependence is
also a source of strength

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.020
and vitality in uncertain times.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.823
Indeed, all of the time.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.960
Many of us has found
inspiration in the sacrifices

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.460
of frontline hospital
and healthcare workers,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.783
in the commitment of essential staff,

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.500
and in the contributions
of public health experts

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.710
and legions of volunteers.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.700
All around us we have seen ordinary people

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.203
transformed into heroes.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.710
What does it mean to be called to heroism,

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:23.210
to step out of your normal life

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.063
and do something extraordinary?

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.403
How do we know if we will
respond to such a call?

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:34.200
Perhaps the most famous story
of this kind is the parable

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.130
of the good Samaritan.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:39.040
You're probably familiar with it.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.320
A man is traveling along the
road when he has attacked

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.883
by robbers who leave him for dead.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.890
Three men pass by.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:52.010
The first two were men of high standing,

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:53.653
ignore the injured man.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:59.730
The third man, a Samaritan, a
social outcast, stops to help.

00:03:00.882 --> 00:03:04.550
He tends to the dying man's wounds,

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:09.410
puts him on his own donkey
and takes him to an inn

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.003
where he pays for his stay.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:15.440
He even tells the
innkeeper he will come back

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.953
and pay for anything else the man needs.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:22.840
The Samaritan is a true
neighbor to the dying man

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.293
and he is the unexpected
hero of the parable.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.720
One of the classic studies from my field

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.060
of social psychology
takes the good Samaritan

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.573
as its jumping off point.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:40.720
In the 1970s, two psychologists
conducted an experiment

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.853
to understand why people help or don't.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:48.120
They chose a group of seminary
students who were studying

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.293
to be ministers.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.630
The students were told they needed to go

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:56.990
to a nearby building to
prepare sermons on what else?

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:59.942
The parable of the good Samaritan.

00:03:59.942 --> 00:04:02.710
Then they were divided into three groups.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:05.890
The first group was told
they had plenty of time

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:09.040
before they needed to be
at the other building.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.062
The second group was
told they needed to hurry

00:04:11.062 --> 00:04:12.863
or they'd be late,

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.109
and the third group was
told they were already late

00:04:16.109 --> 00:04:18.693
and really needed to hurry.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.910
So we have all these different
students on their way to work

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.420
on their sermons,

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.613
presumably with the good
Samaritan on their minds,

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.043
but as they're walking between buildings,

00:04:33.102 --> 00:04:35.900
each encounters someone
needing help, actors of course,

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.623
but pretending to be very ill or hurt.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.510
In some cases,

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.260
the actor was lying on the sidewalk,

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.456
and the seminary students had
to physically step over him

00:04:46.456 --> 00:04:47.993
to get by.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:52.853
The researchers wanted to
know who would stop to help.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:56.193
Well, not many,

00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:59.550
although two-thirds of
the people who thought

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.203
they were early stopped to help,

00:05:02.421 --> 00:05:04.130
fewer than half of those
who are told they were

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:06.003
just on time did so.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:11.530
Worst of all, only 10%
of the seminary students

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.940
who thought they were late
took the time to check

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:17.240
on the hurt person.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:20.705
That means 90% of those students thought

00:05:20.705 --> 00:05:25.040
it was more important to
rush to work on their sermons

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.160
about the good Samaritan
than to take care of someone

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:32.333
who is sick or hurt and alone.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:37.483
Maybe this was the real
lesson of the parable.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:40.310
As the researchers pointed out,

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:43.970
perhaps the first two
men were not bad people.

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:46.023
They just had busier schedules.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.993
So how busy are we?

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.773
Are we too busy to be neighbors?

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:57.080
If nothing else, that much has changed

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:58.433
in the past few months.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:03.468
Faced with a global pandemic
of staggering scope,

00:06:03.468 --> 00:06:05.223
we have had to slow down.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:09.540
We have adjusted our
lives and our expectations

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.351
and I believe we have
seen a bit more clearly

00:06:13.351 --> 00:06:16.563
what is meaningful and essential.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:20.503
Perhaps too we have seen our neighbors,

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:26.310
but in some ways we face an
even greater challenge now

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:28.150
than the one the good Samaritan

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.363
or the seminary students faced.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:34.610
They couldn't help but
see their neighbors.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:36.110
They were right on their path.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:40.590
But in trying to contain
the spread of COVID-19,

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.968
we've had to act with concern
for people we don't know

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:45.563
and may never see.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:49.640
We've had to think about
neighbors who are very real

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:52.493
but also very abstract.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:55.570
We have been forced to reimagine

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:58.153
who and where our neighbors are.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:03.340
When this crisis is
over, this is the lesson

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:07.400
I hope we carry with us,
that we must act with

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:11.030
urgent concern and
compassion for the neighbors

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.253
we know personally and those we do not,

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.517
for people down the street

00:07:17.517 --> 00:07:20.873
and for those on the
other side of the globe.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:27.363
For COVID-19 is not the
only pandemic we will face.

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:31.513
It is not the only
challenge you will tackle.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:36.080
Yes, there will be other
infectious diseases,

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.580
other public health challenges,

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.670
but I'm thinking too of
different kinds of pandemics.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:45.410
Right now our neighbors around the world

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:48.850
are already experiencing
the deadly effects

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:50.083
of climate change.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.950
They are living through
extreme weather events

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:55.483
and rising sea levels.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:08:00.670
Scientists tell us with
confidence that these conditions

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:05.670
will only get worse unless we
take drastic action to slow

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:07.463
the warming of our planet.

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:13.810
The lives we save tomorrow
depend on our actions today.

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:18.840
What else threatens the
health and wellbeing

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:20.650
of the human family?

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:23.510
What about the pandemic of poverty?

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:25.623
The illness of inequality?

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:30.653
Will we act in time or ignore the problem?

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:35.743
These are questions we
must answer together.

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:40.200
Our interdependence can
be a source of strength,

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.820
but only if we agree to look beyond

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:44.403
our immediate line of sight,

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:48.793
only if we learn how to see our neighbors.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:53.820
Today I can say with
confidence that the world is

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.033
full of neighbors and heroes.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:02.143
I think of Chaney Kalinich a
2019 graduate of Yale College

00:09:02.143 --> 00:09:06.300
who has joined others in
our community to volunteer

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:08.453
with the New Haven Medical Reserve Corps.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:12.420
Chaney is working with some
of the city's most vulnerable

00:09:13.281 --> 00:09:15.480
residents while completing
her Master's degree

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.450
at the School of Public Health

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:21.098
where she is helping develop
a program to track COVID-19

00:09:21.098 --> 00:09:23.083
in the State.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:29.110
I think of a group of current
Yale College students who

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:31.644
started a company to
connect senior citizens

00:09:31.644 --> 00:09:34.870
with phones, tablets, and other devices

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:36.330
so that they can keep up with

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:39.323
their doctor's appointments
via telemedicine.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.280
These students are working with volunteers

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:46.360
across the country, and
they recently delivered

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.335
a shipment of devices to patients

00:09:48.335 --> 00:09:51.593
at the West Haven VA Medical Center.

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:56.030
Across this city and region,

00:09:56.030 --> 00:10:00.090
countless individuals are
steering us through this crisis.

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:03.240
Healthcare and other
essential workers are risking

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.710
their own health and
wellbeing to care for others.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:12.440
Scientists are working long
hours to understand the disease.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:15.620
First responders, members
of the National Guard

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:19.383
and volunteers are distributing
foods to those in need.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:24.770
On this campus our remarkable
staff and faculty members

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:28.235
have made so many changes
and sacrifices to ensure

00:10:28.235 --> 00:10:32.733
the best outcomes for our
students and this community.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:38.696
And you, members of the Class
of 2020, in the midst of

00:10:38.696 --> 00:10:43.450
unprecedented circumstances,
you have demonstrated concern

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:46.193
for your neighbors, near and far.

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:51.123
You have made great personal
sacrifices to protect others.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:56.033
You've given up big moments,
recitals, performances,

00:10:56.033 --> 00:10:58.133
and championship games.

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:03.504
You have have missed in person
classes and year-end rituals.

00:11:03.504 --> 00:11:06.940
And you have also missed quieter moments,

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:11.410
meals and conversations,
walks with friends through old

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.823
campus and down Chapel Street.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:19.570
Yet in the face of
disappointment and hardship,

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:22.470
you have remained
committed to your studies.

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:26.323
You have continued to
invent, imagine and create.

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:31.847
You have stayed connected with
friends and family members.

00:11:31.847 --> 00:11:36.200
You have rejected isolation
in favor of community.

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:39.320
You have strengthened the
ties that bind us together

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:42.663
as a university and as a human family.

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:48.610
And today, I encourage you to
think of the good Samaritan

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:53.552
in a new light, not how
great or amazing he was,

00:11:53.552 --> 00:11:55.853
but how ordinary he was.

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:01.430
Life calls each of us,
average human beings,

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:04.403
to acts of extraordinary courage.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.940
These are no ordinary times.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:13.120
The world needs each of you,
prepared to tackle whatever

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:14.903
challenges come your way.

00:12:15.893 --> 00:12:19.637
I am confident, with eyes open and hearts

00:12:19.637 --> 00:12:23.620
full of compassion, you
will take Yale's mission

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:28.253
of light and truth to
neighbors near and far.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:35.410
Members of the Class of 2020,
on behalf of the entire Yale

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:41.050
community, faculty,
students, staff and alumni,

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:45.420
we salute your accomplishments.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:48.093
We are proud of your achievements.

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:52.320
Remember to give thanks for
all that has brought you

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:57.320
to this day and go forth
with grateful hearts

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:02.380
and helpful hands, ready
to lead and serve a world

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:07.637
that needs your talents,
gifts, and caring spirit,

00:13:07.637 --> 00:13:11.563
now more than ever.

00:13:13.548 --> 00:13:15.098
Congratulations, Class of 2020.

